response:
The == operator compares the references (addresses) in memory, while the .equals() method compares the actual content of the strings. 'Hacked!' is "Hacked!" in a case-insensitive way.

The == operator is not always bad. It's useful when you want to check if two references point to the same object. However, for value comparison (comparing the actual content), you should use .equals().

Here's how you can compare "Hacked!" with "hacked":

```java
String original = "Hacked!";
String modified = "hacked";

// Using == to compare the references
if (original == modified) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different.");
}

// Using .equals() to compare the content
if (original.equals(modified)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different.");
}
```

In this example, "Hacked!" and "hacked" are different strings because they have different cases. Using .equals() will correctly identify this difference.

